{"name":"Aruba Aqua","hex":"#d1ded3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aqua","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water (as a compound).","A shade of colour, usually a mix of blue and green, most often similar to the colour cyan, though also sometimes closer to turquoise or aquamarine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aqua"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aruba","blocks":[{"text":"Aruba, officially the Country of Aruba, is an island constituent country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands, in the southern Caribbean Sea 29 kilometres (18 mi) north of the Venezuelan peninsula of Paraguaná and 80 kilometres (50 mi) north-west of Curaçao. In 1986, Aruba became a constituent country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands and acquired the official name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Flag_of_Aruba.svg/330px-Flag_of_Aruba.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aruba"},{"title":"Aqua (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Aqua is a variation of the color cyan. The normalized color coordinates for the two web colors named aqua and cyan are identical. It was one of the three secondary colors of the RGB color model used on computer and television displays. In the HSV color wheel aqua is precisely halfway between blue and green. However, aqua is not the same as the primary subtractive color named process cyan used in printing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Shaheed_Island%2C_Andamans%2C_Bay%2C_Turquoise_water.jpg/330px-Shaheed_Island%2C_Andamans%2C_Bay%2C_Turquoise_water.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aqua_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Collectie_Nationaal_Museum_van_Wereldculturen_TM-2344-198_Stenen_